## STEP 4: INSTALLING THE SOFTWARE ON THE PI

> **What you'll do:** Copy the project files to the Pi and run a single setup command that installs everything the system needs to work.

> **What you need for this step:**
> - SSH access to the Pi (from Step 2)
> - The project files from this repository (the `project/` folder)

---

### Part A: Copy the Project Files to the Pi

> The `project/` folder in this repository contains all the code. You need to copy it to the Pi's `~/thesis/` directory.
>
> **What does `~/thesis/` mean?** The `~` symbol is a shortcut for the home directory (`/home/pi/`). So `~/thesis/` means `/home/pi/thesis/` — this is where all the project files will live on the Pi.

1. **On your laptop**, open a terminal (Command Prompt, PowerShell, or Terminal)

2. Navigate to where you downloaded/cloned this repository. For example:
   ```
   cd C:\Users\YourName\Downloads\Thesis-Configuration
   ```

3. **Create the thesis directory on the Pi** (from your laptop):
   ```
   ssh pi@trio.local "mkdir -p ~/thesis"
   ```

4. **Copy all project files to the Pi:**
   ```
   scp -r project/* pi@trio.local:~/thesis/
   ```
   Enter the password (`raspberrypi`) when prompted.

   > **What is `scp`?** It stands for "Secure Copy" — it copies files from your laptop to the Pi (or vice versa) over the network.
   >
   > **What does `-r` mean?** It means "recursive" — copy all files and subfolders, not just the top level.
   >
   > **What does `project/*` mean?** Copy everything inside the `project/` folder.
   >
   > **What does `pi@trio.local:~/thesis/` mean?** Copy it to the `~/thesis/` directory on the Pi, logged in as user `pi`.

   > **You should see** each file being copied:
   > ```
   > requirements.txt          100%  400     5.2KB/s   00:00
   > setup.sh                  100% 1.2KB   15.0KB/s   00:00
   > settings.py               100% 2.1KB   26.5KB/s   00:00
   > ...
   > ```

---

### Part B: Run the Setup Script (One Command!)

5. **SSH into the Pi:**
   ```
   ssh pi@trio.local
   ```

6. **Go to the project directory:**
   ```
   cd ~/thesis
   ```

7. **Run the setup script:**
   ```
   bash setup.sh
   ```

   > **What does this script do?** It automates everything:
   > 1. Installs system-level libraries the Pi needs (for the camera, OpenCV, etc.)
   > 2. Creates a **Python virtual environment** (an isolated space for our Python packages so they don't conflict with other software)
   > 3. Installs all the Python packages listed in `requirements.txt`
   > 4. Creates all the project directories (`logs/`, `data/`, `models/`, etc.)
   > 5. Verifies that everything installed correctly

   ⏳ **This takes about 5–10 minutes.** You'll see progress messages like:
   ```
   [1/5] Installing system dependencies...
         Done.
   [2/5] Setting up Python virtual environment...
         Created venv at /home/pi/thesis/venv
   [3/5] Installing Python packages...
         Done.
   [4/5] Creating project directories...
         Done.
   [5/5] Verifying installation...

     cv2 (OpenCV)      : 4.x.x
     minimalmodbus      : 2.x.x
     pyserial           : 3.x.x
     ...

   ==================================================
    Setup complete!
    Activate with:  source venv/bin/activate
    Run with:       python3 src/main.py --once
   ==================================================
   ```

   > ✅ If you see **"Setup complete!"** — everything installed successfully.
   >
   > ❌ If you see errors, check that the Pi has internet access: `ping -c 3 google.com`

---

### Part C: Activate the Virtual Environment

8. **Activate the virtual environment:**
   ```
   source venv/bin/activate
   ```

   > **What is a virtual environment?** It's like a separate "bubble" for Python packages. The packages we installed (OpenCV, minimalmodbus, etc.) live inside this bubble and don't affect the rest of the Pi's system. You need to activate it every time you open a new terminal session.

   > **How do you know it's active?** Your terminal prompt will change from:
   > ```
   > pi@trio:~/thesis $
   > ```
   > to:
   > ```
   > (venv) pi@trio:~/thesis $
   > ```
   > Notice the `(venv)` at the beginning — that means the virtual environment is active.

---

### ✅ Checkpoint

You've now:
- ✅ Copied all project files to the Pi
- ✅ Installed all system and Python dependencies
- ✅ Created the project directory structure
- ✅ Verified the installation

> **Remember:** Every time you SSH into the Pi and want to run the project, you need to activate the virtual environment first:
> ```
> cd ~/thesis && source venv/bin/activate
> ```

